<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Vichy France</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1944 CE · capital Vichy</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The authoritarian French State of Marshal Pétain, which collaborated with Nazi Germany and helped deport Jews from France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the German victory in 1940 and the armistice of 22 June, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was divided into an occupied north and an unoccupied south. On 10 July the parliament meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> voted full powers to Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-third-republic">French Third Republic</a> and creating the French State.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Pétain</a> led a '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-nationale">National Revolution</a>' under the motto Work, Family, Fatherland, abolishing parliament and promoting conservative Catholic values. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> chose collaboration after Pétain met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Hitler</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montoire-sur-le-loir">Montoire</a> in 1940. Its own anti-Jewish laws and its police's role in round-ups, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vel-dhiv-roundup">Vel d'Hiv</a> in 1942, contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of about 76,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied the south in November 1942, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> little power. After the Allied landings of 1944 the regime collapsed; its leaders were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> in Germany. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Pétain</a> was sentenced to death, commuted to life imprisonment, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval">Pierre Laval</a> was executed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vichy-france.jpg" alt="Vichy France" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vichy</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe Pétain, Pierre Laval, François Darlan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French State, État français</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=vichy-france" aria-label="Vichy France on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4761 554 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vichy France lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5081 810L5077 1108M5081 810L5054 728M5081 810L5300 622M5081 810L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5077 1108h0M5054 728h0M5300 622h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5081 810h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5081 810h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vichy. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=vichy-france">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
